@h1 Your first document h1@

The fastest way to understand Wit is to watch a plain text file turn into a
finished document. No preamble, no boilerplate, no template to fill in — you
write sentences, run one command, and a styled page comes out. Let's do exactly
that.

@h2 Write something h2@

Open a file called @code hello.wit code@ and just write. A blank line starts a
new paragraph; nothing else is required.

@@@pre
The keeper had not spoken aloud in eleven days.

He had not noticed, until his own voice surprised him.

The lamp burned steady through the storm — the kind of weather
that, in another life, would have called for company.
pre@@@

Three paragraphs, zero markup. This is a complete, valid Wit document already.

@h2 Build it h2@

Turn the file into a web page with one command:

@@@pre
wit build hello.wit -o hello.html
pre@@@

Open @code hello.html code@ in a browser. It already looks like a real document —
generous margins, a readable serif, sensible spacing — because the default HTML
output is a complete, self-contained styled file. You didn't write a line of CSS,
and you don't need to. That batteries-included theme is the point: a first draft
should look finished enough to send.

@h2 Add a little structure h2@

Now reach for a heading and some emphasis. Add a first line and mark two words:

@@@pre
@h1 The Lamp Keeper h1@

The keeper had not spoken aloud in *eleven days*. He had not
noticed, until his own _voice_ surprised him.
pre@@@

Run the same build again and refresh. The @@@code @h1 … h1@ code@@@ became a real
heading, @code *eleven days* code@ became bold, and @code _voice_ code@ became
italic. Everything you didn't mark stayed exactly as you typed it. That is the
rhythm of Wit: prose by default, structure precisely where you put it.

@h2 Build to PDF h2@

Change the output extension and you get a paginated, print-ready PDF:

@@@pre
wit build hello.wit -o hello.pdf
pre@@@

Under the hood, Wit renders that same styled HTML and paginates it through a
headless copy of Chrome. So PDF has one requirement the other formats don't: a
system Chrome or Chromium must be installed. If none is found, the build tells
you plainly:

@@@pre
wit build: no headless Chrome/Chromium found for PDF output.
Install Google Chrome, or point WIT_CHROME at a browser executable.
pre@@@

The fix is in the message — install Chrome, or set the @code WIT_CHROME code@
environment variable to a browser you already have. HTML and Markdown never need
a browser; only PDF does.

@h2 The other outputs h2@

The @code -o code@ extension decides the format, and you have four ways to emit:

@ul
@li @strong No output flag strong@ — the HTML prints to your terminal (handy for piping). li@
@li @code -o page.html code@ — a complete, styled, self-contained document. li@
@li @code -o page.md code@ — clean Markdown, the same source rendered for a different pipeline. li@
@li @code -o page.pdf code@ — the paginated PDF above. li@
ul@

Two flags fine-tune the HTML. @code --fragment code@ emits only the bare
@@@code <article> code@@@ with no page wrapper, for pasting into a site you already
have. @code --raw code@ emits a full page with a minimal reset instead of the
built-in theme, for when you want to bring your own CSS. Reach for these when
you're embedding; the plain default is right for everything else.

@h2 The whole loop h2@

Editing Wit is a three-beat cycle: @strong edit strong@ the file, run
@strong build strong@, @strong refresh strong@ the output. That's it.

@aside(class note) 💡 There is no @code --watch code@ flag today. The build is
fast enough that re-running it on save feels instant, and if you want automatic
rebuilds you can wrap it in a one-line shell loop:
@code while true; do wit build report.wit -o report.html; sleep 1; done code@.
An auto-watcher is on the wish list, but be honest with yourself: it doesn't
exist yet. aside@

Two more commands smooth the loop. @code wit check hello.wit code@ reports parse
and reference errors without building anything — run it when a build looks wrong.
And @code wit fmt hello.wit code@ tidies a file's indentation without changing a
character of meaning (add @code -w code@ to write the changes back).

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li @strong Waiting for @code --watch code@ to rebuild for you. strong@ It isn't there — re-run the build, or use the shell loop above. li@
@li @strong Expecting PDF with no browser installed. strong@ PDF needs a system Chrome or @code WIT_CHROME code@; read the fallback message and follow it. li@
@li @strong Marking a word and seeing nothing change. strong@ The marks wrap a single token — check @a |href /docs/start/troubleshooting/| Troubleshooting a@ if emphasis or a node isn't taking. li@
ul@

